{"name":"Mayan Blue","hex":"#006b6c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Maya blue","blocks":[{"text":"Maya blue is a unique bright turquoise or azure blue pigment manufactured by cultures of pre-Columbian Mesoamerica, such as the Mayas and Aztecs, during a period extending from approximately the 8th century to around 1860 CE. It is found in mural paintings on architectural buildings, ceramic pieces, sculptures, codices, and even in post-conquest Indochristian artworks and mural decorations."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7a/Azulm6.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maya_blue","wikidata":"Q901499"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Juangerson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ab/Juangerson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Mexican Colonial Painting by Juan Gerson where Maya blue was used","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJuangerson.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Mic_bonampak.gif/330px-Mic_bonampak.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Mic_bonampak.gif/330px-Mic_bonampak.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Microscopic image of a mural at Bonampak","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMic_bonampak.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Mic_teotihuacan.gif/330px-Mic_teotihuacan.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Mic_teotihuacan.gif/330px-Mic_teotihuacan.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Microscopic image of a mural at Teotihuacan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMic_teotihuacan.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/The_Florentine_Codex-_Lunar_Eclipse.tiff/lossy-page1-120px-The_Florentine_Codex-_Lunar_Eclipse.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/The_Florentine_Codex-_Lunar_Eclipse.tiff/lossy-page1-250px-The_Florentine_Codex-_Lunar_Eclipse.tiff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&